\pard\pardeftab720\sl280\sa240\partightenfactor0

\f1 \cf2 \expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
\outl0\strokewidth0 \strokec2 The evidence lies in what the repetitions 
\i change
\i0 , not what they retain. Three specific alterations tell us the purpose.\
\pard\pardeftab720\sl280\sa240\partightenfactor0

\b \cf2 1. The watchman material (vv. 1-9) is re-grounded in the people's own institution.
\b0  Chapter 3 begins directly: 
\f2 \uc0\u1510 \u1465 \u1508 \u1462 \u1492 
\f1  
\f2 \uc0\u1504 \u1456 \u1514 \u1463 \u1514 \u1460 \u1468 \u1497 \u1498 \u1464 
\f1 . Chapter 33 first spends five verses on a hypothetical civic case: 
\f2 \uc0\u1506 \u1463 \u1501 
\f1  
\f2 \uc0\u1492 \u1464 \u1488 \u1464 \u1512 \u1462 \u1509 
\f1  takes 
\f2 \uc0\u1488 \u1460 \u1497 \u1513 \u1473 
\f1  
\f2 \uc0\u1488 \u1462 \u1495 \u1464 \u1491 
\f1  
\f2 \uc0\u1502 \u1460 \u1511 \u1456 \u1510 \u1461 \u1497 \u1492 \u1462 \u1501 
\f1  \'97 a man from among themselves \'97 and 
\i they
\i0  appoint him (
\f2 \uc0\u1493 \u1456 \u1504 \u1464 \u1514 \u1456 \u1504 \u1493 \u1468 
\f1  
\f2 \uc0\u1488 \u1465 \u1514 \u1493 \u1465 
\f1  
\f2 \uc0\u1500 \u1464 \u1492 \u1462 \u1501 
\f1  
\f2 \uc0\u1500 \u1456 \u1510 \u1465 \u1508 \u1462 \u1492 
\f1 ). Only then does verse 7 apply the office to Ezekiel. In chapter 3 the watchman commission is Ezekiel's private ordination, spoken to him alone at his call, and the audience never hears it. In 33 it's spoken 
\f2 \uc0\u1488 \u1462 \u1500 
\f1  
\f2 \uc0\u1489 \u1456 \u1468 \u1504 \u1461 \u1497 
\f1  
\f2 \uc0\u1506 \u1463 \u1502 \u1456 \u1468 \u1498 \u1464 
\f1  \'97 to be delivered 
\i to the people
\i0 . The purpose of the repetition is thus not to recommission Ezekiel but to make the 
\i audience
\i0  accept the logic of the office: a watchman's warning transfers responsibility to hearers (
\f2 \uc0\u1491 \u1464 \u1468 \u1502 \u1493 \u1465 
\f1  
\f2 \uc0\u1489 \u1456 \u1512 \u1465 \u1488 \u1513 \u1473 \u1493 \u1465 
\f1  
\f2 \uc0\u1497 \u1460 \u1492 \u1456 \u1497 \u1462 \u1492 
\f1 , v. 4). The civic analogy exists so the exiles will concede the principle from their own military practice before discovering it applies to their standing before the sword YHWH brings. That's the same rhetorical trap structure as chapters 15, 17, 23 \'97 secure agreement to the obvious case first.\

\b 2. The chapter 18 material is re-keyed to the audience's new speech.
\b0  In 18 the people's slogan was the sour-grapes proverb \'97 a deflection 
\i outward
\i0  to the fathers. In 33:10 the quoted speech is new: 
\f2 \uc0\u1508 \u1456 \u1468 \u1513 \u1464 \u1473 \u1506 \u1461 \u1497 \u1504 \u1493 \u1468 
\f1  
\f2 \uc0\u1493 \u1456 \u1495 \u1463 \u1496 \u1465 \u1468 \u1488 \u1514 \u1461 \u1497 \u1504 \u1493 \u1468 
\f1  
\f2 \uc0\u1506 \u1464 \u1500 \u1461 \u1497 \u1504 \u1493 \u1468 
\f1  
\f2 \uc0\u1493 \u1468 \u1489 \u1464 \u1501 
\f1  
\f2 \uc0\u1488 \u1458 \u1504 \u1463 \u1495 \u1456 \u1504 \u1493 \u1468 
\f1  
\f2 \uc0\u1504 \u1456 \u1502 \u1463 \u1511 \u1460 \u1468 \u1497 \u1501 
\f1  
\f2 \uc0\u1493 \u1456 \u1488 \u1461 \u1497 \u1498 \u1456 
\f1  
\f2 \uc0\u1504 \u1460 \u1495 \u1456 \u1497 \u1462 \u1492 
\f1  \'97 "our transgressions are on us, we rot in them, how can we live?" That 
\f2 \uc0\u1504 \u1456 \u1502 \u1463 \u1511 \u1460 \u1468 \u1497 \u1501 
\f1  is Leviticus 26:39 again, the same word from 24:23. The people have moved from denial to despair; they now 
\i concede
\i0  the entire case of chapters 1-24 and draw a fatalistic conclusion from it. So the repetition of the two-way doctrine (righteous who turns dies, wicked who turns lives) serves the opposite pastoral function it served in 18. There it broke false security; here it breaks false doom. The material is identical because the doctrine is symmetric; the purpose differs because the audience has crossed to the other error. Confirmation: 33 adds the oath and doubled imperative absent from 18's ending \'97 
\f2 \uc0\u1495 \u1463 \u1497 
\f1  
\f2 \uc0\u1488 \u1464 \u1504 \u1460 \u1497 
\f1 ... 
\f2 \uc0\u1513 \u1473 \u1493 \u1468 \u1489 \u1493 \u1468 
\f1  
\f2 \uc0\u1513 \u1473 \u1493 \u1468 \u1489 \u1493 \u1468 
\f1 ... 
\f2 \uc0\u1493 \u1456 \u1500 \u1464 \u1502 \u1464 \u1468 \u1492 
\f1  
\f2 \uc0\u1514 \u1464 \u1502 \u1493 \u1468 \u1514 \u1493 \u1468 
\f1  \'97 the divine plea intensified precisely where despair, not complacency, is the disease.\

\b 3. The dated hinge (vv. 21-22) tells us 
\i\b0 when
\i0\b  this restatement becomes operative.
\b0  The 
\f2 \uc0\u1508 \u1464 \u1468 \u1500 \u1460 \u1497 \u1496 
\f1  arrives: 
\f2 \uc0\u1492 \u1467 \u1499 \u1456 \u1468 \u1514 \u1464 \u1492 
\f1  
\f2 \uc0\u1492 \u1464 \u1506 \u1460 \u1497 \u1512 
\f1 . Ezekiel's mouth is opened, fulfilling 24:25-27 exactly. The repetition of 3 and 18 material is placed immediately 
\i before
\i0  this notice, which I take as deliberate arrangement: the book is saying that the watchman office and the turn-and-live doctrine are the terms on which the opened mouth will now speak. Before the fall, warning served to establish guilt retrospectively ("they will know a prophet was among them" \'97 and note 33:33 repeats exactly that formula from 2:5, now applied to the 
\i restoration
\i0  words they hear but don't do). After the fall, the same office serves life prospectively. The content repeats; the temporal position converts its function from indictment to invitation.\
So my claim: 
\b Ezekiel 33 repeats in order to re-issue prior material under changed conditions of hearing \'97 the fall of the city transforms warning-as-evidence into warning-as-lifeline, and the text marks this by (a) making the private commission public, (b) answering despair where 18 answered presumption, and (c) dating the whole to the arrival of the news.
\b0  The repetition is not redundancy but re-inauguration: chapter 33 is a second call narrative for the second half of the book.\
One piece of counter-pressure I'd raise against my own reading: vv. 23-29 (the ruins-dwellers claiming Abraham's inheritance) and vv. 30-33 (the exiles treating Ezekiel as a 
\f2 \uc0\u1513 \u1460 \u1473 \u1497 \u1512 
\f1  
\f2 \uc0\u1506 \u1458 \u1490 \u1464 \u1489 \u1460 \u1497 \u1501 
\f1 , a love-song entertainer) show that the changed condition did 
\i not
\i0  produce changed hearing \'97 presumption survives in the land, and aesthetic consumption replaces obedience among the exiles. So the chapter itself refuses the tidy "now they'll finally listen" reading. The opened mouth speaks to people who hear beautifully and do nothing. That complicates "invitation" \'97 the invitation is real, but 33:33 predicts recognition will again come only by fulfillment. Which purpose dominates, I'd argue, is settled by verse 11's oath: God's disposition is the fixed point; the audience's response remains the variable, exactly as the watchman frame requires.\
Where do you want to press \'97 the civic-appointment framing, or the despair/presumption inversion?
