\f1 \cf2 \expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
\outl0\strokewidth0 \strokec2 I think there may be other kinds of evidence besides change - but change is the obvious tractable kind of evidence, and an excellent starting point to dig into so we have a map of the chapter to orient ourselves before using other, harder to use evidence.\
You raise good evidence for that rhetorical trap structure. But a rhetorical trap implies a need for such a trap; what is the shape of that need implied here?\
As for rotting away, by disease or like the diseased, I think conceding the entire case of chapters 1 through 24 is a strong claim that would need more evidence. Even simply conceding the claim of Leviticus 26:39, now fulfilled, is a strong claim, even though that covenant curse is referenced rather directly. One or both might be the case of course. Also, being more specific about the evidence for those claims may help us be more specific about what we mean by both breaking and false doom.\
Moreover the evidence of the additions in verse 11 are important enough to be worth a dive both into the exact text there and the connections to other parts of the text and other parts of the rhetoric.\
Now, the date is certainly evidence, and the events dated are at least somewhat of a hinge. And the chapter does not lack for signs of arrangement, so the order is telling us something. But we have several overlapping problems that we need to resolve to use this evidence.\
First, turn and live has been either explicit or implicit in quite a few of the oracles placed before this marker; less so afterwards. Just the previous chapter had some explicit pull on the hearts of the nations, and an implicit reference to the repentance the exile might bring of Deuteronomy 30.\
Second, even in this chapter, the hearts of the people are still far from ready to hear. There are certainly some restoration oracles in later chapters. But what immediately follows is not precisely invitation. You note this yourself. But while verse 11 communicates a disposition, Deuteronomy 30 already communicated that disposition. And the logic of both chapters requires a repentant people for restoration - a repentance that does not seem forthcoming from this particular vantage point. Or, to put it another way, the LORD's disposition to show mercy is quite explicitly put alongside the LORD's determination to punish the wicked, which should hardly have been surprising after the LORD revealed himself to Moses as merciful and gracious, but not clearing the guilty.\
Third, we know the sign from before: the fall of Babylon. But what sign now comes? The restoration? If so, what evidence in the text is there for this? Who will know? And if restoration, why verse 29?\
I ask these questions and more not only to disagree, but to see if we can find from the text the principles by which to build our interpretation.\
To do that, I think we need to ask what Ezekiel does when he springs the trap of verses 2 through 6. What message does he press, given that opportunity? There was just a war, a war the exile's nations lost. But who, now, is the watchman warning? And who, now, chose the watchman? And who, now, does the watchman warn of?
\f0 \cf0 \kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 \outl0\strokewidth0 \
\pard\tx720\tx1440\tx2160\tx2880\tx3600\tx4320\tx5040\tx5760\tx6480\tx7200\tx7920\tx8640\pardirnatural\partightenfactor0
