\
\pard\pardeftab720\sl280\sa240\partightenfactor0

\f1 \cf2 \expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
\outl0\strokewidth0 \strokec2 <notes>Concede the overclaim on v.10; the pronouns in vv. 2-9 are where the real work is. 
\f2 \uc0\u1502 \u1460 \u1502 \u1462 \u1468 \u1504 \u1460 \u1468 \u1497 
\f1  in v.7 is the hinge.</notes>\
Let me answer your three-part question directly, because I think the answers dismantle part of my earlier reading.\
\pard\pardeftab720\sl280\sa240\partightenfactor0

\b \cf2 Who chose the watchman?
\b0  In the civic case, the people do: 
\f2 \uc0\u1493 \u1456 \u1500 \u1464 \u1511 \u1456 \u1495 \u1493 \u1468 
\f1  
\f2 \uc0\u1506 \u1463 \u1501 
\f1  
\f2 \uc0\u1492 \u1464 \u1488 \u1464 \u1512 \u1462 \u1509 
\f1 ... 
\f2 \uc0\u1493 \u1456 \u1504 \u1464 \u1514 \u1456 \u1504 \u1493 \u1468 
\f1  
\f2 \uc0\u1488 \u1465 \u1514 \u1493 \u1465 
\f1  
\f2 \uc0\u1500 \u1464 \u1492 \u1462 \u1501 
\f1  
\f2 \uc0\u1500 \u1456 \u1510 \u1465 \u1508 \u1462 \u1492 
\f1 . In the application, this reverses: 
\f2 \uc0\u1510 \u1465 \u1508 \u1462 \u1492 
\f1  
\f2 \uc0\u1504 \u1456 \u1514 \u1463 \u1514 \u1460 \u1468 \u1497 \u1498 \u1464 
\f1  \'97 
\i I
\i0  have given you. The trap gets the audience to accept an office whose legitimacy they'd grant because 
\i they'd
\i0  have chosen its holder \'97 and then reveals the appointment was never theirs. They cannot dismiss Ezekiel as a watchman they didn't hire. The parable's concession ("obviously a duly appointed watchman's warning binds us") holds even though the appointing party turns out to be YHWH.\

\b Who does the watchman warn of?
\b0  This is the sharpest change, and I passed over it. Verse 2: 
\f2 \uc0\u1488 \u1462 \u1512 \u1462 \u1509 
\f1  
\f2 \uc0\u1499 \u1460 \u1468 \u1497 
\f1  
\f2 \uc0\u1488 \u1464 \u1489 \u1460 \u1497 \u1488 
\f1  
\f2 \uc0\u1506 \u1464 \u1500 \u1462 \u1497 \u1492 \u1464 
\f1  
\f2 \uc0\u1495 \u1464 \u1512 \u1462 \u1489 
\f1  \'97 the hypothetical sword is 
\i already
\i0  "I bring." And verse 7: 
\f2 \uc0\u1493 \u1456 \u1492 \u1460 \u1494 \u1456 \u1492 \u1463 \u1512 \u1456 \u1514 \u1464 \u1468 
\f1  
\f2 \uc0\u1488 \u1465 \u1514 \u1464 \u1501 
\f1  
\f2 \uc0\u1502 \u1460 \u1502 \u1462 \u1468 \u1504 \u1460 \u1468 \u1497 
\f1 . The min is doing double duty \'97 "warn them 
\i from
\i0  Me" (on my behalf) shades into "warn them 
\i of
\i0  Me." Chapter 3:17 has the identical phrase, but there it sat inside a private commission before a siege. Now it's published to people who just watched a sword come and go. The civic watchman scans the horizon for an enemy army. Ezekiel scans for YHWH. The enemy the shofar announces is the One who appointed the watchman \'97 the same collapse chapter 13 performed on the whitewashers, where the storm they failed to warn of 
\i was
\i0  YHWH.\

\b Who is being warned, now?
\b0  Here's the shape of the need you asked for. The exiles' plausible post-fall inference is: 
\i the sword has come and gone; we are the survivors; the danger is behind us.
\i0  The war is over; their side lost; watchmen are peacetime-irrelevant. The trap exists because the text must overturn the audience's tense. The civic parable is set in war precisely to smuggle the watchman logic past people who think the war ended. Verse 4's sword that "comes and takes him" isn't Nebuchadnezzar's \'97 that one already took whom it took. The sword still coming is death-in-iniquity, individual, present tense: 
\f2 \uc0\u1492 \u1493 \u1468 \u1488 
\f1  
\f2 \uc0\u1489 \u1463 \u1468 \u1506 \u1458 \u1493 \u1465 \u1504 \u1493 \u1465 
\f1  
\f2 \uc0\u1504 \u1460 \u1500 \u1456 \u1511 \u1464 \u1495 
\f1 . The fall of Jerusalem didn't discharge the danger; it removed the last excuse for misreading who wields it.\
This is why I now think you're right to reject my "conceded the entire case" claim about verse 10. Look at what they actually still say in vv. 17 and 20: 
\f2 \uc0\u1500 \u1465 \u1488 
\f1  
\f2 \uc0\u1497 \u1460 \u1514 \u1464 \u1468 \u1499 \u1461 \u1503 
\f1  
\f2 \uc0\u1491 \u1462 \u1468 \u1512 \u1462 \u1498 \u1456 
\f1  
\f2 \uc0\u1488 \u1458 \u1491 \u1465 \u1504 \u1464 \u1497 
\f1  \'97 
\i after
\i0  hearing turn-and-live restated. People who had conceded the case of chapters 1-24 could not still call the way unbalanced. So verse 10 is not confession; it's a 
\i new deflection wearing confession's vocabulary
\i0 . They quote Leviticus 26:39's 
\f2 \uc0\u1504 \u1502 \u1511 
\f1  back \'97 evidence they've registered that the curse-sequence is where they are \'97 but they deploy it as fatalism: "we rot; therefore how can we live; therefore turning is pointless." It has the grammar of penitence and the function of refusal. That's more precise than "despair": it's the covenant curse acknowledged as 
\i fact
\i0  while rejected as 
\i summons
\i0 . Leviticus 26:40 (confession) is the next verse in the sequence they're quoting, and they conspicuously don't take it. The doctrine of vv. 12-16 breaks this not by comforting but by removing the premise \'97 the rot is not load-bearing; no accumulated past, righteous or wicked, determines the day of turning.\

\b On verse 11's additions.
\b0  Three features absent from ch. 18's ending: the oath 
\f2 \uc0\u1495 \u1463 \u1497 
\f1  
\f2 \uc0\u1488 \u1464 \u1504 \u1460 \u1497 
\f1 , the doubled imperative 
\f2 \uc0\u1513 \u1473 \u1493 \u1468 \u1489 \u1493 \u1468 
\f1  
\f2 \uc0\u1513 \u1473 \u1493 \u1468 \u1489 \u1493 \u1468 
\f1 , and the vocative 
\f2 \uc0\u1489 \u1461 \u1468 \u1497 \u1514 
\f1  
\f2 \uc0\u1497 \u1460 \u1513 \u1456 \u1474 \u1512 \u1464 \u1488 \u1461 \u1500 
\f1  closing the plea. The oath matters most, and your Exodus 34 frame is the right one \'97 with this addition: the chapter contains 
\i two
\i0  
\f2 \uc0\u1495 \u1463 \u1497 
\f1  
\f2 \uc0\u1488 \u1464 \u1504 \u1460 \u1497 
\f1  oaths. Verse 11: as I live, I do not delight in the wicked's death. Verse 27: as I live, those in the ruins will fall by the sword. Ezekiel puts both halves of Exodus 34:6-7 under the same self-oath in the same chapter \'97 mercy sworn, non-clearing-of-the-guilty sworn, neither cancelling the other. The disposition of v. 11 is not new information (Deut 30 had it, as you say); what's new is its 
\i formal elevation to oath
\i0  at the precise moment the audience is using God's judgments as evidence against God's mercy. The oath answers a specific epistemic crisis: they now have empirical proof God kills; verse 11 puts the counter-truth beyond empirical doubt by staking it on God's life.\

\b On the hinge problem and your third question \'97 what sign now comes?
\b0  I'll defend a narrower version of my earlier claim. Verse 33: 
\f2 \uc0\u1493 \u1468 \u1489 \u1456 \u1489 \u1465 \u1488 \u1464 \u1492 \u1468 
\f1  
\f2 \uc0\u1492 \u1460 \u1504 \u1461 \u1468 \u1492 
\f1  
\f2 \uc0\u1489 \u1464 \u1488 \u1464 \u1492 
\f1  
\f2 \uc0\u1493 \u1456 \u1497 \u1464 \u1491 \u1456 \u1506 \u1493 \u1468 
\f1  
\f2 \uc0\u1499 \u1460 \u1468 \u1497 
\f1  
\f2 \uc0\u1504 \u1464 \u1489 \u1460 \u1497 \u1488 
\f1  
\f2 \uc0\u1492 \u1464 \u1497 \u1464 \u1492 
\f1  
\f2 \uc0\u1489 \u1456 \u1514 \u1493 \u1465 \u1499 \u1464 \u1501 
\f1 . The feminine "it" has no near antecedent \'97 the nearest candidate is the 
\i content of the words
\i0  they hear and don't do (v. 32), which from this point forward is predominantly restoration. So the verification structure of 2:5 is reused with the referent swapped: before, fulfillment-in-destruction would prove the prophet; now, fulfillment-in-what-he-speaks-next will. That's the evidence that something turned at v. 21 \'97 not the audience's hearts (vv. 30-33 foreclose that), not the availability of mercy (constant since Moses), but the 
\i direction of the word awaiting verification
\i0 .\
And verse 29 fits rather than fights this, because vv. 23-29 address a different audience with a different tense. The ruins-dwellers' claim \'97 Abraham was one, we are many, the land is ours \'97 is presumption 
\i in the land
\i0 , and for them the sword is still future; their recognition formula (v. 29) is destruction-shaped because their oracle is chapter 1-24 material still executing. The chapter holds three audiences in sequence: exiles tempted to fatalism (turn and live), remnant tempted to presumption (the sword continues), exiles tempted to aestheticism (the word will verify itself). The watchman office covers all three because its logic is indifferent to which error the hearer prefers \'97 the shofar sounds; the blood settles on whoever heard.\
So, revised claim: the repetition's purpose is not "warning becomes invitation." It is that 
\b the fall of Jerusalem changed what the audience could evade, and the chapter re-issues the old material to close the new evasions
\b0  \'97 the appointment they didn't make, the sword they think is past, the rot they treat as fate, the God they've reduced to the judgments they witnessed.\
What I can't yet settle from the text: whether 
\f2 \uc0\u1502 \u1460 \u1502 \u1462 \u1468 \u1504 \u1460 \u1468 \u1497 
\f1  in v. 7 should bear the full weight I'm putting on it, or whether "on my behalf" exhausts it. The chapter 13 parallel inclines me to the heavier reading. Do you take the min as source, object, or deliberately both?
