\f1 \cf2 \expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
\outl0\strokewidth0 \strokec2 In that verse 7 is the same language as from chapter 3, and chapter 3 was followed by several chapters quite insistent on 'I am against you' and elaborations on that idea, I think we have sufficient evidence that "on my behalf" does not exhaust the word.\
As for those remaining in the land, I think we need to be more precise: what is their claim, and their theory behind that claim?\
In what is it wrong? What wrong assumptions is it grounded in?\
What is the LORD\'92s answer - for word and deed are the same.\
Before and after that, I think we see a possibly different wrong assumption behind complaint rather than repentance. Blaming others, rather than repenting - why?\
These answers may take us closer to understanding the rhetoric of this chapter.\
