\f1 . The argument is a 
\i kal vachomer
\i0  from arithmetic: if one man inherited, how much more many. The theory beneath it: the land grant runs on descent and headcount. Possession is a status conferred by lineage from Abraham, and survival-in-the-land is itself evidence of election \'97 we're still here; the exiles were removed; therefore the inheritance devolved to us.\

\b Where it's wrong \'97 and the precision matters.
\b0  They cite Abraham's 
\i inheritance
\i0  while abstracting away everything by which Abraham related to the Giver. Genesis 15:6 stands behind the grant; Genesis 18:19 makes it explicit: "I have known him 
\i so that
\i0  he will command his children to keep the way of YHWH, to do righteousness and justice (
\f2 \uc0\u1510 \u1456 \u1491 \u1464 \u1511 \u1464 \u1492 
\f1  
\f2 \uc0\u1493 \u1468 \u1502 \u1460 \u1513 \u1456 \u1473 \u1508 \u1464 \u1468 \u1496 
\f1 ), 
\i so that
\i0  YHWH may bring upon Abraham what He has spoken." The grant has a 
\f2 \uc0\u1500 \u1456 \u1502 \u1463 \u1506 \u1463 \u1503 
\f1  structure. They quote the conclusion and delete the purpose clauses. Note also: 
\f2 \uc0\u1502 \u1460 \u1513 \u1456 \u1473 \u1508 \u1464 \u1468 \u1496 
\f1  
