\f1  is exactly the phrase vv. 14, 16, 19 just used as the content of turning. The chapter's arrangement makes the ruins-dwellers fail the test its first half defined.\
The deeper wrong assumption: 
\b the land is treated as an object that can be possessed, rather than a place where one lives before YHWH.
\b0  That's why the LORD's answer is a conduct list, not a counter-genealogy. He doesn't say "you are not Abraham's seed." He says: you eat over the blood, you lift eyes to idols, you shed blood \'97 
\f2 \uc0\u1493 \u1456 \u1492 \u1464 \u1488 \u1464 \u1512 \u1462 \u1509 
\f1  
\f2 \uc0\u1514 \u1460 \u1468 \u1497 \u1512 \u1464 \u1513 \u1473 \u1493 \u1468 
\f1 ? Twice, and the second time the list escalates: 
\f2 \uc0\u1506 \u1458 \u1502 \u1463 \u1491 \u1456 \u1514 \u1462 \u1468 \u1501 
\f1  
\f2 \uc0\u1506 \u1463 \u1500 \u1470 \u1495 \u1463 \u1512 \u1456 \u1489 \u1456 \u1468 \u1499 \u1462 \u1501 
\f1  \'97 "you stand on your sword." That phrase answers the Abraham citation directly: Abraham received the land as gift while owning no soil in it but a purchased grave; these claim it by the sword they stand on. They've replaced the mode of tenure (gift held in righteousness) with the mode of seizure \'97 which is the nations' mode, which is why Leviticus 18:24-28's logic follows: the land vomited out nations who did these things, and it does not check pedigree. Every item on the list is either from the Leviticus 18-20 expulsion-grounds or from Ezekiel 18/22's bloodshed catalog. The land was never theirs to inherit apart from those terms; quantity is simply the wrong category.\

\b Word and deed the same:
\b0  the answer is itself an oath \'97 the chapter's second 
\f2 \uc0\u1495 \u1463 \u1497 
\f1  
\f2 \uc0\u1488 \u1464 \u1504 \u1460 \u1497 
\f1  (v. 27) \'97 distributing sword, beast, and pestilence across ruins, field, and strongholds. The triad is Leviticus 26's and Ezekiel 5-6's, i.e., the covenant curse continuing to execute 
\i within the land
\i0  after the city fell. This settles what v. 29 is doing: the recognition formula is destruction-shaped because for this audience the destruction isn't finished. Their theory said the sword's passing confirmed their title; the oath says the sword hasn't passed. And note what 
\f2 \uc0\u1490 \u1456 \u1468 \u1488 \u1493 \u1465 \u1503 
\f1  
\f2 \uc0\u1506 \u1467 \u1494 \u1464 \u1468 \u1492 \u1468 
\f1  (v. 28) echoes \'97 Leviticus 26:19, "I will break the pride of your strength." The answer never leaves the covenant text they've stopped reading.\

\b Now your harder question \'97 why blame rather than repent, on both sides of this section?
\b0  The exiles in vv. 10, 17, 20 and the ruins-dwellers in v. 24 look opposite \'97 despair versus presumption \'97 but I think the text shows one root: 
\b both parties reason from position rather than from relation.
\b0  The ruins-dwellers: our position (in the land, numerous, descended) secures us. The exiles: our position (under the curse, rotting in 
\f2 \uc0\u1508 \u1456 \u1468 \u1513 \u1464 \u1473 \u1506 \u1461 \u1497 \u1504 \u1493 \u1468 
\f1 ) dooms us. Both treat their standing before YHWH as a settled 
\i state
\i0  determined by accumulated facts. And vv. 12-16 attack exactly that shared premise from both directions at once: the righteous man's stored righteousness will not deliver him \'97 
\f2 \uc0\u1500 \u1465 \u1488 
\f1  
\f2 \uc0\u1514 \u1463 \u1510 \u1460 \u1468 \u1497 \u1500 \u1462 \u1504 \u1468 \u1493 \u1468 
\f1  
\f2 \uc0\u1489 \u1456 \u1468 \u1497 \u1493 \u1465 \u1501 
\f1  
\f2 \uc0\u1508 \u1460 \u1468 \u1513 \u1456 \u1473 \u1506 \u1493 \u1465 
\f1  \'97 and the wicked man's stored wickedness will not trip him \'97 
\f2 \uc0\u1500 \u1465 \u1488 
\f1  
\f2 \uc0\u1497 \u1460 \u1499 \u1464 \u1468 \u1513 \u1461 \u1473 \u1500 
\f1  
\f2 \uc0\u1489 \u1464 \u1468 \u1492 \u1468 
\f1  
\f2 \uc0\u1489 \u1456 \u1468 \u1497 \u1493 \u1465 \u1501 
\f1  
\f2 \uc0\u1513 \u1473 \u1493 \u1468 \u1489 \u1493 \u1465 
\f1 . No accumulated position, good or bad, is load-bearing on the day of turning. The doctrine isn't primarily about fairness; it's the demolition of position-theology in every form.\
Why is blame preferred to repentance? Because both blame-forms are 
\i cheaper
\i0  \'97 they require no present action. If the way of the Lord is unbalanced (vv. 17, 20), the fault is located in God and nothing follows for me. If the rot is fate (v. 10), nothing 
\i can
\i0  follow for me. If the land is mine by headcount (v. 24), nothing 
\i need
\i0  follow for me. All three are theories whose function is to make 
\f2 \uc0\u1513 \u1473 \u1493 \u1468 \u1489 \u1493 \u1468 
\f1  inapplicable. And this is why the watchman frame governs the whole chapter: the shofar transfers responsibility to the hearer and removes every intermediate excuse \'97 
\f2 \uc0\u1491 \u1464 \u1468 \u1502 \u1493 \u1465 
\f1  
\f2 \uc0\u1489 \u1456 \u1512 \u1465 \u1488 \u1513 \u1473 \u1493 \u1465 
\f1  
\f2 \uc0\u1497 \u1460 \u1492 \u1456 \u1497 \u1462 \u1492 
\f1 . The three quoted speeches (v. 10, v. 17/20, v. 24) are three attempts to hand the blood back \'97 to fate, to God's injustice, to inherited title \'97 and each oracle-unit returns it: 
\f2 \uc0\u1488 \u1460 \u1497 \u1513 \u1473 
\f1  
\f2 \uc0\u1499 \u1460 \u1468 \u1491 \u1456 \u1512 \u1464 \u1499 \u1464 \u1497 \u1493 
\f1  
\f2 \uc0\u1488 \u1462 \u1513 \u1456 \u1473 \u1508 \u1465 \u1468 \u1496 
\f1  
\f2 \uc0\u1488 \u1462 \u1514 \u1456 \u1499 \u1462 \u1501 
\f1  (v. 20).\
Which suggests the rhetoric of the chapter is more unified than my three-audiences reading allowed: 
\b one office (watchman), one demand (turn), one shared disease (standing on position \'97 sword, lineage, ledger, or curse \'97 instead of standing before the living God who swears He wants you alive).
\b0  Even vv. 30-33 fit: sitting before the prophet as audience is itself a position \'97 
\f2 \uc0\u1506 \u1463 \u1502 \u1460 \u1468 \u1497 
\f1 , they come "as my people" come \'97 occupied 
\i instead of
\i0  doing. Hearing has become one more place to stand.\
