\f1 \cf2 \expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
\outl0\strokewidth0 \strokec2 Possess is a word the LORD himself used, even in Genesis 15, though your point about Abraham not owning soil and receiving as gift is not in any way thus diminished. And your Genesis 18 point stands as well. Not that I think we have proof that Ezekiel referred to Genesis 18 here, as those words are used many places. But Genesis 18 gives us a clear statement of the LORD's purpose, and the people are clearly against the LORD in this matter. So the LORD is against them. Which is precisely the logic Ezekiel has used so many times in previous chapters. The people are rebels, not inheritors, and will be treated as such.\
But I wonder - is there a way to look more deeply into the possible connection to the Leviticus expulsion grounds and the Ezekiel bloodshed catalog? Whether from the messages from Ezekiel 18 and 22, or more detailed verbal links, or a better understanding of Ezekiel's rhetorical purpose here, I think we can make a much better claim.\
That unification may be a good start - but it needs first specificity, second evidence.\
As for verse 31, chapter 22 also has its vocabulary borrowed. Greed too, in addition to lust, is a repeat. But while to some extent, lust was a metaphor in chapter 23, greed is named for what it is - though a similar metaphor may also be true. It is the heart which must turn back. But Ezekiel does not give further details on greed; his focus is on lust. But notice that lust is not what the people think is going on. Or more, agree together out loud what is going on. Come, hear, from the LORD. But inside, in mouth and heart, lust and greed. And thus, somehow hearing the word of the LORD and yet that word is inert, playing on the emotions, the aesthetics. But treated as if it were without force, as if not from a prophet, as if it were not a command from the LORD who rules all. Like it were from a singer, with the purpose of being pleasing.
