\f1 \cf2 \expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
\outl0\strokewidth0 \strokec2 I would not go so far as to say Leviticus 18 is the only Torah text with expulsion mechanics, but it is the one Ezekiel is working with. Deuteronomy 9:4, for instance, is not directly in view. Though Deuteronomy 27 and 28 may be in view. Not because its list of people who are cursed is the same, but because of the structure, what is cursed, and then the emphasis on doing or not doing what the LORD said. But also, because there was already reason to believe Deuteronomy 30 was in play, and the choose life or death ideas of Ezekiel 33 only reinforce that. But that is far far less sure than Leviticus 18, and even were the Deuteronomy reference also in view, it does not push against your conclusions from Leviticus 18.\
I would add moreover to the Ezekiel 18 stream. Ezekiel 18 was, in a way, interpreting Torah. It calls to the revealed character of the LORD. And Ezekiel 22 goes further; it calls to reason about proper conduct, throwing at the practical atheism behind so much wrongdoing the simple fact that all lives belong to the LORD, and to treat human life as worthless is both incorrect and wicked. So yes, very much a standard they could have heard, should have known, should have kept to. And Ezekiel 22 very much cuts against the sort of ruthlessness of power, that might makes right or at least makes one's actions unassailable, because there is a judge above all human institutions one might use in ways that do not respect human life.\
And against the reliance on the sword, Ezekiel 22:14 asks a heart wrenching question: "Can your courage endure, or can your hands be strong, in the days that I shall deal with you?" Before and after that question, the LORD says he will act. And then where will reliance on the sword be?\
And so, as you noted, the one who stands on the sword will fall by it.\
As for aesthetic reception, I think that no intention of contact with reality is part of the message. Such that those who have no lust as such are by no means free of this error. Many know, and even have aesthetic appreciation for the words of Ezekiel. But to come at them as only a scholar, only from the outside, only looking at how the words appear, only perceiving the associations the words bring but not the LORD who intends them as message and warning and a way of life - these are all too common. Such a sacred text - but are you set apart for the service of the one who spoke it? Such beautiful words - but are you drawn to the source of their beauty? Such moving images - but are you moved to obey? These questions have not become less relevant as the Bible has become well known as an important beautiful book.\
And so, this misclassification of the word, if the evidence points towards that symmetry you name being real and central to the chapter, is far from irrelevant. Moreover, people still fall into despair, fate, entitlement, bored entertainment seeking.\
Yet while I find your chapter seven question interesting - your reference leapt out at me, as it were - and it certainly fits what we have been saying, I am not sure that chapter fits here. We have there a relentless focus on the day, the time, coming, and the end.\
Instead, I turn anew to the text, with verse 33 in mind. You shall surely die, the sentence announced. The contrast between whether the word of the people referenced in the chapter will come to pass, and whether the word of the LORD will come to pass. The LORD's oath. The opposite of the sentence on the wicked, pronounced on the one for whom it is true, in the statues of life he walks, he will surely live. The charge, as in the material repeated from earlier in Ezekiel, that the LORD's ways are arbitrary - and the charge countered by the certainty of the LORD's principled ways. And verse 33 itself - knowledge results, despite their every intention of anything but.\
And so, having gone to the text, I do not see the people saying 'not yet', but being so detached that they do not even see the question of an end. And yet, despite an approach that skirts around reality, the people will know.
